# CSSLv3 / SIGIL at the Asymptote — A Frontier Map for a Sovereign Language

This document is a frontier map, not a roadmap. It is structured as
the user requested: hardware → representation → computational model →
synthesis. Every section names the primary research, characterizes the
design space, and flags where claims are cited fact versus
extrapolation. The constraint set is taken as given: sovereignty (no
permanent foreign substrates like LLVM, MLIR, Slang), the Prime
Directive (consent-as-OS, capability-secure, no surveillance/coercion
features), a solo developer working with an AI fleet, and an unbounded
time horizon.

The thesis that emerges across all forty-four sub-questions is this:
**the frontier is not in any single dimension but in the
orthogonalities that have never been combined.** Content-addressed
code (Unison) has never been combined with interaction-net runtimes
(HVM2). Algebraic effects (Koka) have never been combined with
choreographic projection (Pirouette). Probabilistic semantics (Gen)
have never been combined with linear capabilities (Pony) and
projectional editing (Hazel). Rank-polymorphic arrays (Remora) have
never been the *base type system* of a dependently-typed prover
(Lean 4). Each pair is a research paper waiting to be written; the
cartesian product is a language that has never existed. The sections
below identify the load-bearing primitives in each line and the
adjacencies that compound them.

A consistent thread: **the canonical form must not be text, must not
be SSA-over-scalars, and must not be a single-tier IR.** Every
frontier in this document points away from "source file → AST → SSA →
machine code" and toward "content-addressed hypergraph → multi-tier
projection → multi-substrate execution." That is the structural
conclusion.

# Layout of the arc

- **Part I** (UPG_02–UPG_04): Hardware-Level Frontiers
- **Part II** (UPG_05–UPG_06): Representation-Level Frontiers
- **Part III** (UPG_07–UPG_09): Computational-Model-Level Frontiers
- **Part IV** (UPG_10–UPG_11): Synthesis, Vision, Cartesian Product
- **Appendix** (UPG_12): Engineering notes, sovereignty path, honest
  bottom line

# How to read this

If you only have time for one file: read UPG_11 (the cartesian
product). It is the synthesis. Everything else is the underlying
research that justifies the claims there.

If you have time for two files: add UPG_08 (the type-system
unification — graded modal types as the load-bearing mechanism).
Almost every Prime-Directive-relevant claim in the synthesis grounds
out in graded modal types plus capabilities.

If you have time for three: add UPG_06 (hypergraph canonical form +
self-modifying AST + content addressing + AI-native source). This is
the *representation* frontier that makes the synthesis possible.

The remaining files fill in the hardware, the orthogonal computational
models, the landscape of current ambitious languages, and the
engineering bootstrap path.
